hi there steve coffin here today i want  to talk about  a subject that comes up from time to  time it's kind of two subjects in one  i often get asked um  can we learn more than one language at  the same time  and another question is you know  how difficult is it to learn related or  similar languages well first of all i'm  going to follow this video with a video  where i speak in both ukrainian and  russian with subtitles i had someone  uh familiar with both languages provide  us with subtitles so i'm putting those  up  and you can hear the differences between  ukrainian  and russian  um  obviously if you study a language that's  similar to a language that you already  know you have a tremendous advantage  because chances are the vocabulary is  going to be similar  the grammar is going to be similar  similar  and obviously  as i've said many times the more similar  a new language is to a language you  already know the easier it is so i'm  struggling with arabic right now where  there are  practically no  cognates there are no words that i  recognize from other languages maybe  when i go to study farsi i'll see some  arabic  words in the farsi and that might help  me so the more similar they are the  better  but there are some traps  okay first of all you can't assume that  you just kind of tweak your  spanish and you'll have portuguese uh  when i first went at portuguese i tried  that i bought a book on the differences  between  spanish and portuguese in terms of  vocabulary and stuff like that  and it really didn't help me that much  because those you know those obvious  differences where they say you know this  in spanish and that in portuguese that's  a small percentage of the vocabulary you  actually have to get used to using all  of the language and understanding all of  the language so just zooming at least  this was my experience just trying to  shortcut the process by just picking out  those few words that are different and  then pretending you're speaking in this  other language i find i found that it  didn't work  i spent some time on some portuguese  phrases in this living language course  that i bought and i listened a lot  jogging around and listening and went to  portugal and i people just switched to  english so i couldn't really communicate  i didn't know what they were saying  so the second time i went at it much  more thoroughly thoroughly linked style  lots of listening and reading immersing  myself into the language  sort of projecting myself as a speaker  of portuguese and then i was able to do  much better and it really didn't matter  whether to me whether i was listening to  brazilian portuguese or portuguese  portuguese i was putting myself into  another language environment  and we have many members here at linc  who are portuguese probably more brazil  not portuguese brazilian probably we  have more portuguese speakers than  spanish speakers i don't know for sure  but they of course want to learn spanish  and sometimes i get questions about that  and i always say you know the big thing  is don't kind of hang back  in the comfort of your portuguese  pronunciation the same is true in  reverse of course  try to project yourself as an  argentinian or a colombian or a spaniard  or something try to really project  yourself into that environment so the  same with the slavic languages the first  language i learned was was russian  obviously i spent a lot of time with  russian then i did czech  and then i came to  ukrainian and even polish and by the way  this morning i had a one-hour chat with  a polish tutor from italki in polish we  spoke in polish now  i am sure that a significant percentage  of the words i used were either  ukrainian or czech or russian but we  were communicating that's another  advantage when you are in closely  related languages if the goal is  communication in a way it doesn't matter  if you're mixing spanish with portuguese  or italian or  polish with ukrainian or russian  people are going to understand you so if  the main goal is to communicate  and of course you understand them  because you studied those languages so  your comprehension is  usually a lot better than your ability  to deliver the language but you're still  able to communicate  but the funny thing is again i'm kind of  rambling a bit here but the funny thing  is that i felt in my polish conversation  that while i probably made more mistakes  than the last time i spoke polish  the overall experience was easier  because i had in the intervening period  had studied ukrainian so that my  comfort level with slavic languages  in total  was  higher so i felt quite comfortable  and i would throw out a word and you  know if it's if it's uh peras and polish  and zara's and ukrainian or vice versa i  can't remember half the time it didn't  matter because we were communicating and  i felt comfortable and so feeling  comfortable  and confident is extremely important in  learning any language  but  i guess where i want to come to in all  this is there has to be a desire to  learn that other language there has to  be a desire to become one of them and  very often you see people speakers of  similar languages and particularly  native speakers who don't force  themselves  and i was i listened to a lot of  portuguese podcasts  and i would hear portuguese people speak  spanish and they would try to speak  spanish like spaniards and they sounded  spanish and very often i would hear  spanish people  who would  probably  change a few words hablar became falar  and desert instead of defer and that was  about it but for the rest of time they  were just speaking spanish so and and  i've noticed for example mandarin  speakers who come who move to vancouver  let's say  young people young kids who want to  connect with cantonese speakers here in  vancouver they learn  in no time because actually if the  language is that close you can learn  but older people they simply don't want  to let go of their mandarin of the  pronunciation of the comfort and so they  will just speak  mandarin or spanish or russian  uh so that typically it's the more  dominant language group that is  unwilling to project itself into that  call it less widely spoken language  group so  i guess to summarize it's a tremendous  advantage to learn  related languages  easy to confuse them but it doesn't  matter because you're still  communicating in my view  but you should force yourself to make a  special effort to get out of the comfort  zone of the dom of the language that  where you are stronger  in order to really  penetrate immerse yourself get into  sound natural in  the  next language within that language group  and it is fun  uh you know i've done you know they're  not the same language family but chinese  helped me with japanese helped me with  korean obviously the slavic languages i  was able to go from one to the next to  the next  if i ever wanted to learn  uh danish or norwegian it would be a  piece of cake but if i did it i would go  for the danish pronunciation i wouldn't  try to say a few danish words with a  swedish accent i would totally go for it  and the same of course is true for  romance languages and that's why for  example romanian i was able to learn  fairly quick quickly  but i left my italian behind me i became  romanian so there it is a bit of a  ramble on learning uh related languages  and now you can hear me  in ukrainian and uh  russian neither of which i spent a lot  of time at over the last year or two but  you'll certainly get the sense that my  russian is stronger than my ukrainian  and my russian creeps into my ukrainian  okay thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

